ong)((name - *namep) + 1)`. So `m->_len` should never exceed `MAX_PACKET_LEN` because of the checks.\n\nHmm, but what about the case where `name` is advanced but the function returns false? Let me check: if the function returns false, `m->_len` is NOT updated. So `m->_len` stays the same. But `name` was already advanced within the function. The next call to `_label` would start from the same `m->_len`, so `name` would be at the same position.\n\nActually wait, I think I need to look at this more carefully. Let me re-read the `_label` function:\n\n```c\n*namep = name = (char *)m->_packet + m->_len;\n```\n\nThen in the loop:\n```c\nmemcpy(name, label + 1, labelLen);\nname[labelLen] = '.';\nname += labelLen + 1;\n```\n\nAnd after the loop:\n```c\n*name = '\\0';\n...\nm->_len += (unsigned long)((name - *namep) + 1);\n```\n\nSo `name` advances by `labelLen + 1` for each label. The check `if ((unsigned char*)name + labelLen > m->_packet + MAX_PACKET_LEN)` should prevent overflow.\n\nBut what if `labelLen` is 0? Then `memcpy(name, label + 1, 0)` does nothing, `name[0] = '.'` writes a dot, and `name += 1`. The check `if ((unsigned char*)name + 0 > m->_packet + MAX_PACKET_LEN)` would be `if ((unsigned char*)name > m->_packet + MAX_PACKET_LEN)`. If `name` is at `m->_packet + MAX_PACKET_LEN`, this is false, so we proceed. Then `name[0] = '.'` writes at `m->_packet + MAX_PACKET_LEN`, which is 1 byte past!\n\nBut wait, `labelLen` is checked: `if (labelLen > 63) return false;`. And `labelLen = (unsigned char)*label`. So `labelLen` can be 0. A zero-length label is the root label, which terminates a domain name. But the code checks `if (*label == 0) { break; }` BEFORE checking the label length. So a zero-length label would cause a break before the memcpy.\n\nHmm, but what about the case where `*label` is between 1 and 63, and `name` is at `m->_packet + MAX_PACKET_LEN - 1`? Then `(unsigned char*)name + labelLen` could be `MAX_PACKET_LEN - 1 + 63 = MAX_PACKET_LEN + 62`, which is > `M